---
import Site from "../layouts/Site.astro";
import { latestRelease, RELEASES_URL } from "../lib/release";

const release = await latestRelease();
const platforms = [
  { key: "mac", name: "macOS", note: "Apple silicon, macOS 12.3 or later. No Intel build yet." },
  { key: "windows", name: "Windows", note: "Windows 10 (1803) or later, 64-bit." },
  { key: "linux", name: "Linux", note: "x86_64. Screen sharing goes through PipeWire and the desktop portal." },
] as const;
---
<Site
  title="OpenDesk: self-hosted, end-to-end encrypted remote desktop"
  description="Open-source remote desktop for Windows, macOS, Linux and the browser. You run the relay; the screen and input are encrypted end to end so the relay can't read them."
>
  <section class="hero">
    <div class="wrap hero__grid">
      <div class="hero__copy">
        <p class="eyebrow">Open source · Self-hosted · Beta</p>
        <h1>Remote desktop through a relay that can't see your screen.</h1>
        <p class="lede">
          OpenDesk lets you view and control another computer over the internet.
          You run the relay. The two computers encrypt the screen, input, sound
          and files between themselves, so the relay only passes along bytes it
          can't read.
        </p>
        <div class="actions">
          <a class="button button--primary" href="#download">Download</a>
          <a class="button button--secondary" href="/connect/">Open the browser viewer</a>
        </div>
        <p class="hint">
          For Windows, macOS and Linux. The browser viewer controls a computer
          that runs the app, without installing anything.
        </p>
      </div>

      <figure class="mock" aria-labelledby="mock-caption">
        <div class="mock__window" aria-hidden="true">
          <div class="mock__bar"><span></span><span></span><span></span></div>
          <div class="mock__body">
            <div class="mock__card">
              <span class="mock__label">Your ID</span>
              <span class="mock__id">482 913 075</span>
              <span class="mock__label">Session password</span>
              <span class="mock__field">••••••••••</span>
            </div>
            <div class="mock__card">
              <span class="mock__label">Control a remote device</span>
              <span class="mock__field mock__field--id">731 204 586</span>
              <span class="mock__field">••••••••••</span>
              <span class="mock__button">Connect</span>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="mock__toolbar">
            <span class="mock__dot"></span>
            <span class="mock__mono">731 204 586</span>
            <span class="mock__badge">Direct · 18 ms</span>
            <span class="mock__tools">Fullscreen Clipboard Sound Files Record</span>
            <span class="mock__disconnect">Disconnect</span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <figcaption id="mock-caption">
          The home screen shows this computer's ID and a one-time password.
          During a session, the toolbar shows whether the connection is direct or relayed.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band" aria-labelledby="how-title">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2 id="how-title">How a session works</h2>
      <figure class="diagram">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 720 250" role="img" aria-labelledby="diagram-title diagram-desc">
          <title id="diagram-title">Host, relay and viewer</title>
          <desc id="diagram-desc">
            The host and the viewer each open an outbound wss connection on port 443 to the relay.
            They try a direct UDP path between themselves. If that fails, the same encrypted
            traffic goes through the relay, which cannot decrypt it.
          </desc>
          <defs>
            <marker id="arrow" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto-start-reverse">
              <path d="M0 0 10 5 0 10z" class="diagram__arrowhead" />
            </marker>
          </defs>
          <rect x="10" y="150" width="190" height="80" rx="10" class="diagram__box" />
          <text x="105" y="185" class="diagram__title">Host</text>
          <text x="105" y="208" class="diagram__note">shares its screen</text>

          <rect x="520" y="150" width="190" height="80" rx="10" class="diagram__box" />
          <text x="615" y="185" class="diagram__title">Viewer</text>
          <text x="615" y="208" class="diagram__note">app or browser</text>

          <rect x="265" y="10" width="190" height="80" rx="10" class="diagram__box diagram__box--relay" />
          <text x="360" y="45" class="diagram__title">Your relay</text>
          <text x="360" y="68" class="diagram__note">forwards sealed bytes</text>

          <path d="M150 150 Q170 90 262 62" class="diagram__line" marker-end="url(#arrow)" />
          <path d="M570 150 Q550 90 458 62" class="diagram__line" marker-end="url(#arrow)" />
          <text x="70" y="110" class="diagram__label">outbound wss://</text>
          <text x="650" y="110" class="diagram__label">outbound wss://</text>

          <path d="M204 190 H516" class="diagram__line diagram__line--direct" marker-start="url(#arrow)" marker-end="url(#arrow)" />
          <text x="360" y="180" class="diagram__label diagram__label--accent">direct UDP when the network allows</text>
          <text x="360" y="212" class="diagram__label">ChaCha20-Poly1305 either way</text>
        </svg>
      </figure>
      <ol class="steps">
        <li>
          <h3>Share an ID and password</h3>
          <p>The computer you want to reach shows a 9-digit ID and a password that changes after every session.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h3>Agree on a key</h3>
          <p>The other side enters both. SPAKE2 turns the password into a key that only the two computers know. The password never crosses the network.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h3>Connect the shortest way</h3>
          <p>They try a direct UDP path first. If a firewall blocks it, the same encrypted traffic goes through the relay instead.</p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="features-title">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2 id="features-title">What you get</h2>
      <ul class="features">
        <li>
          <h3>Your own relay</h3>
          <p>One small Rust service. The Docker Compose setup puts Caddy in front, which gets the HTTPS certificate for you.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h3>End-to-end encryption</h3>
          <p>A key per session from SPAKE2, ChaCha20-Poly1305 on every frame and keystroke. TLS protects the connection to the relay on top of that.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h3>No open ports</h3>
          <p>Both computers connect out to the relay on port 443. No port forwarding, no inbound firewall rules.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h3>Direct when it can be</h3>
          <p>ICE finds a direct UDP path between the computers. The session toolbar says Direct or Relayed and shows the round trip.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h3>The usual tools</h3>
          <p>System sound, text clipboard sync, file transfer with a consent prompt, session recording, and a picker for each monitor.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h3>Unattended access</h3>
          <p>A fixed password for your own machines, an optional code from an authenticator app, and start at sign-in so a restart doesn't lock you out.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h3>Open source</h3>
          <p>AGPL-3.0. Rust for the relay, crypto and capture; Tauri and React for the app. Read it, build it, run your own.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h3>Any desktop, plus a browser</h3>
          <p>One app hosts and views on Windows, macOS and Linux. A browser can view and control without installing anything.</p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band" id="download" aria-labelledby="download-title">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2 id="download-title">Download</h2>
      <p class="section-lede">
        {release
          ? <>Newest build: <a href={release.url}>{release.tag}</a>{release.prerelease ? " (beta)" : ""}. </>
          : <>Get the newest build from the <a href={RELEASES_URL}>releases page</a>. </>}
        Install it on both computers. The app connects to the project's test relay out of the box;
        you can <a href="/docs/self-hosting/">point it at your own</a>.
      </p>
      <div class="downloads">
        {platforms.map((platform) => {
          const files = release?.[platform.key] ?? [];
          return (
            <section class="download" aria-labelledby={`dl-${platform.key}`}>
              <h3 id={`dl-${platform.key}`}>{platform.name}</h3>
              <p>{platform.note}</p>
              <ul class="download__files">
                {files.length > 0
                  ? files.map((file, index) => (
                      <li>
                        <a class={index === 0 ? "button button--primary" : "button button--quiet"} href={file.url}>{file.label}</a>
                        <span class="download__detail">{file.detail}</span>
                      </li>
                    ))
                  : <li><a class="button button--primary" href={RELEASES_URL}>Get it on GitHub</a></li>}
              </ul>
            </section>
          );
        })}
      </div>
      <div class="notice">
        <p>
          <strong>These builds are not code-signed.</strong> The first time you open the app,
          macOS Gatekeeper and Windows SmartScreen will warn you. <a href="/docs/getting-started/">Getting started</a>
          shows how to get past each warning. All builds and their changelogs are on the
          <a href={RELEASES_URL}>releases page</a>.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="limits-title">
    <div class="wrap two-col">
      <div>
        <h2 id="limits-title">Before you rely on it</h2>
        <ul class="plain-list">
          <li>OpenDesk is in beta. Most testing has happened on macOS; Windows and Linux hosting is less mature.</li>
          <li>The browser viewer always goes through the relay and has no sound, file transfer, clipboard sync or recording.</li>
          <li>Linux hosts on Wayland have limited input support.</li>
          <li>The relay can't read your session, but it does see who connects to whom, when, and how much data flows. <a href="/docs/security/">Security model</a></li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h2>Run a relay in a few minutes</h2>
        <p>On any server with Docker and a DNS name:</p>
        <pre class="code"><code>git clone https://github.com/treeot/opendesk.git
cd opendesk
DOMAIN=relay.example.com docker compose up -d</code></pre>
        <p>Then set each app's relay to <code>wss://relay.example.com/ws</code>. <a href="/docs/self-hosting/">Self-hosting guide</a></p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Site>
